1
00:00:05,859 --> 00:00:08,500
chapter 8.

2
00:00:08,500 --> 00:00:13,420
I couldn’t sleep all night; a foghorn was groaning incessantly on the Sound, and I

3
00:00:13,420 --> 00:00:19,180
tossed half sick between grotesque reality and savage, frightening dreams.

4
00:00:19,180 --> 00:00:24,100
Toward dawn I heard a taxi go up Gatsby’s drive, and immediately I jumped

5
00:00:24,100 --> 00:00:29,079
out of bed and began to dress— —I felt that I had something to tell him,

6
00:00:29,079 --> 00:00:34,478
something to warn him about, and mourning would be too late.

7
00:00:34,478 --> 00:00:39,520
Crossing his lawn, I saw that his front door was still open and he was leaning

8
00:00:39,520 --> 00:00:43,359
against the table in the hall, heavy with dejection or sleep.

9
00:00:43,359 --> 00:00:49,779
“Nothing happened,” he said wanley I waited, and about four o’clock she came to the

10
00:00:49,779 --> 00:00:54,040
window and stood there for a minute and then turned out the light.”

11
00:00:54,040 --> 00:00:57,939
His house had never seemed so enormous to me as it did that night when we

12
00:00:57,939 --> 00:01:01,120
hunted through the great rooms for cigarettes.

13
00:01:01,120 --> 00:01:06,279
We pushed aside curtains that were like pavilions, and felt over innumerable feet

14
00:01:06,279 --> 00:01:12,100
of dark wall for electric light switches— —once I tumbled with a sort of splash

15
00:01:12,100 --> 00:01:14,859
upon the keys of a ghostly piano.

16
00:01:14,859 --> 00:01:20,379
There was an inexplicable amount of dust everywhere, and the rooms were musty, as

17
00:01:20,379 --> 00:01:23,139
though they hadn’t been aired for many days.

18
00:01:23,139 --> 00:01:29,379
I found the humidora on an unfamiliar table, with two stale, dry cigarettes inside.

19
00:01:29,379 --> 00:01:35,379
Throwing open the French windows of the drawing room we sat smoking out into the darkness.

20
00:01:35,379 --> 00:01:40,359
“You ought to go away,” I said. “It’s pretty certain they’ll trace your car.”

21
00:01:40,359 --> 00:01:45,818
“Go away now old sport?” “Go to Atlantic City for a week, or up to

22
00:01:45,818 --> 00:01:50,799
Montreal.” He wouldn’t consider it. He couldn’t possibly leave Daisy until he

23
00:01:50,799 --> 00:01:52,898
knew what she was going to do.

24
00:01:52,898 --> 00:01:57,699
He was clutching at some last hope and I couldn’t bear to shake him free.

25
00:01:57,699 --> 00:02:02,738
It was this night that he told me the strange story of his youth with Dan Cody—

26
00:02:02,738 --> 00:02:07,959
—told it to me because “Jay Gatsby” had been broken up like glass against Tom’s

27
00:02:07,959 --> 00:02:13,299
hard malice, and the long secret extravaganza was played out.

28
00:02:13,299 --> 00:02:18,159
I think that he would have acknowledged anything now, without reserve, but he

29
00:02:18,159 --> 00:02:20,559
wanted to talk about Daisy.

30
00:02:20,559 --> 00:02:24,159
She was the first “nice” girl he had ever known.

31
00:02:24,159 --> 00:02:28,898
In various unrevealed capacities he had come in contact with such people,

32
00:02:28,898 --> 00:02:32,799
but always with indiscernible barbed wire between.

33
00:02:32,799 --> 00:02:38,738
He found her excitingly desirable. He went to her house, at first with other

34
00:02:38,738 --> 00:02:42,279
officers from Camp Taylor, then alone.

35
00:02:42,279 --> 00:02:47,318
It amazed him— —he had never been in such a beautiful house before.

36
00:02:47,318 --> 00:02:53,379
But what gave it an air of breathless intensity, was that Daisy lived there—

37
00:02:53,379 --> 00:02:58,478
—it was as casual a thing to her as his tent out at camp was to him.

38
00:02:58,478 --> 00:03:04,238
There was a ripe mystery about it, a hint of bedrooms upstairs more beautiful and

39
00:03:04,238 --> 00:03:10,898
cool than other bedrooms, of gay and radiant activities taking place through its corridors,

40
00:03:10,898 --> 00:03:16,420
and of romances that were not musty and laid away already in lavender

41
00:03:16,420 --> 00:03:23,139
but fresh and breathing and redolent of this year’s shining motor cars and of

42
00:03:23,139 --> 00:03:26,439
dances whose flowers were scarcely withered.

43
00:03:26,439 --> 00:03:33,459
It excited him, too, that many men had already loved Daisy— —it increased her value in his eyes.

44
00:03:33,459 --> 00:03:38,379
He felt their presence all about the house, pervading the air with the shades

45
00:03:38,379 --> 00:03:42,100
and echoes of still vibrant emotions.

46
00:03:42,100 --> 00:03:46,180
But he knew that he was in Daisy’s house by a colossal accident.

47
00:03:46,180 --> 00:03:51,879
However glorious might be his future as Jay Gatsby, he was at present a penniless

48
00:03:51,879 --> 00:03:57,100
young man without a past, and at any moment the invisible cloak of

49
00:03:57,100 --> 00:04:00,100
his uniform might slip from his shoulders.

50
00:04:00,100 --> 00:04:02,620
So he made the most of his time.

51
00:04:02,620 --> 00:04:08,559
He took what he could get, ravenously and unscrupulously— —eventually

52
00:04:08,559 --> 00:04:15,100
he took Daisy one still October night, took her because he had no real right to

53
00:04:15,100 --> 00:04:16,719
touch her hand.

54
00:04:16,719 --> 00:04:21,939
He might have despised himself, for he had certainly taken her under false pretenses

55
00:04:21,939 --> 00:04:26,799
I don’t mean that he had traded on his fan to millions, but he had deliberately

56
00:04:26,799 --> 00:04:31,719
given Daisy a sense of security; he let her believe that he was a person from

57
00:04:31,719 --> 00:04:37,600
much the same stratum as herself— —that he was fully able to take care of her.

58
00:04:37,600 --> 00:04:43,478
As a matter of fact, he had no such facilities— —he had no comfortable family standing

59
00:04:43,478 --> 00:04:48,939
behind him, and he was liable at the whim of an impersonal government to be blown

60
00:04:48,939 --> 00:04:51,699
anywhere about the world.

61
00:04:51,699 --> 00:04:56,559
But he didn’t despise himself and it didn’t turn out as he had imagined.

62
00:04:56,559 --> 00:05:01,299
He had intended, probably, to take what he could and go—

63
00:05:01,299 --> 00:05:06,699
—but now he found that he had committed himself to the following of a grail. He

64
00:05:06,699 --> 00:05:10,898
knew that Daisy was extraordinary, but he didn’t realize just how

65
00:05:10,898 --> 00:05:14,139
extraordinary a “nice” girl could be.

66
00:05:14,139 --> 00:05:21,760
She vanished into her rich house, into her rich, full life, leaving Gatsby— —nothing.

67
00:05:21,760 --> 00:05:27,219
He felt married to her, that was all. When they met again, two

68
00:05:27,219 --> 00:05:32,799
days later, it was Gatsby who was breathless, who was, somehow, betrayed. Her

69
00:05:32,799 --> 00:05:37,659
porch was bright with the bought luxury of Starshine the wicker of the city

70
00:05:37,659 --> 00:05:43,600
squeaked fashionably as she turned toward him and he kissed her curious and lovely mouth.

71
00:05:43,600 --> 00:05:48,100
She had caught a cold, and it made her voice huskier and more charming than

72
00:05:48,100 --> 00:05:53,439
ever, and Gatsby was overwhelmingly aware of the youth and mystery that wealth

73
00:05:53,439 --> 00:05:59,738
imprisons and preserves, of the freshness of many clothes, and of Daisy, gleaming

74
00:05:59,738 --> 00:06:04,719
like silver, safe and proud above the hot struggles of the poor.

75
00:06:04,719 --> 00:06:06,879
“I can’t describe to you how surprised I

76
00:06:06,879 --> 00:06:09,398
was to find out I loved her, old sport. I

77
00:06:09,398 --> 00:06:13,840
even hoped for a while that she’d throw me over, but she didn’t, because she was

78
00:06:13,840 --> 00:06:15,520
in love with me too.

79
00:06:15,520 --> 00:06:19,059
She thought I knew a lot because I knew different things from her…

80
00:06:19,059 --> 00:06:25,359
Well, there I was, way off my ambitions, getting deeper in love every minute, and

81
00:06:25,359 --> 00:06:27,520
all of a sudden I didn’t care. What was

82
00:06:27,520 --> 00:06:31,659
the use of doing great things if I could have a better time telling her what I

83
00:06:31,659 --> 00:06:35,318
was going to do?” On the last afternoon before he went

84
00:06:35,318 --> 00:06:41,139
abroad, he sat with Daisy in his arms for a long, silent time. It was a cold fall

85
00:06:41,139 --> 00:06:46,840
day, with fire in the room and her cheeks flushed. Now and then she moved and he

86
00:06:46,840 --> 00:06:51,699
changed his arm a little, and once he kissed her dark shining hair.

87
00:06:51,699 --> 00:06:55,120
The afternoon had made them tranquil for a while,

88
00:06:55,120 --> 00:07:00,579
as if to give them a deep memory for the long parting the next day promised. They

89
00:07:00,579 --> 00:07:04,359
had never been closer in their month of love and were communicated more

90
00:07:04,359 --> 00:07:09,340
profoundly one with another, than when she brushed silent lips against his

91
00:07:09,340 --> 00:07:17,318
coat’s shoulder or when he touched the end of her fingers, gently, as though she were asleep.

92
00:07:17,318 --> 00:07:19,659
He did extraordinarily well in the war.

93
00:07:19,659 --> 00:07:24,100
He was a captain before he went to the front, and following the Argon battles he

94
00:07:24,100 --> 00:07:28,120
got his majority and the command of the divisional machine guns

95
00:07:28,120 --> 00:07:32,680
After the armistice he tried frantically to get home, but some complication or

96
00:07:32,680 --> 00:07:35,559
misunderstanding sent him to Oxford instead.

97
00:07:35,559 --> 00:07:40,898
He was worried now— —there was a quality of nervous despair in Daisy’s letters.

98
00:07:40,898 --> 00:07:45,459
She didn’t see why he couldn’t come. She was feeling the pressure of the world

99
00:07:45,459 --> 00:07:50,020
outside, and she wanted to see him and feel his presence beside her and be

100
00:07:50,020 --> 00:07:54,639
reassured that she was doing the right thing after all. For Daisy was young and

101
00:07:54,639 --> 00:07:59,318
her artificial world was redolent of orchids and pleasant, cheerful snobbery

102
00:07:59,318 --> 00:08:03,639
and orchestras which set the rhythm of the year, summing up the sadness and

103
00:08:03,639 --> 00:08:09,398
suggestiveness of life in new tunes. All night the saxophones wailed the hopeless

104
00:08:09,398 --> 00:08:14,620
comment of the “Beale Street Blues” while a hundred pairs of Goldman silver

105
00:08:14,620 --> 00:08:17,680
slippers shuffled the shining dust.

106
00:08:17,680 --> 00:08:22,420
At the Gray tea hour there were always rooms that throbbed incessantly with

107
00:08:22,420 --> 00:08:27,639
this low, sweet fever, while fresh faces drifted here and there like rose petals

108
00:08:27,639 --> 00:08:30,639
blown by the sad horns around the floor.

109
00:08:30,639 --> 00:08:34,978
Through this twilight universe Daisy began to move again with the season;

110
00:08:34,978 --> 00:08:39,520
suddenly she was again keeping half a dozen dates a day with half a dozen men,

111
00:08:39,520 --> 00:08:44,199
and drowsing asleep at dawn with the beads and chiffon of an evening dress

112
00:08:44,199 --> 00:08:48,879
tangled among dying orchids on the floor beside her bed.

113
00:08:48,879 --> 00:08:53,559
And all the time something within her was crying for a decision.

114
00:08:53,559 --> 00:08:59,020
She wanted her life shaped now, immediately— —and the decision must be

115
00:08:59,020 --> 00:09:09,279
made by some force— —of love, of money, of unquestionable practicality— —that was close at hand.

116
00:09:09,279 --> 00:09:14,379
That force took shape in the middle of spring with the arrival of Tom Buchanan.

117
00:09:14,379 --> 00:09:18,639
There was a wholesome bulkiness about his person and his position, and Daisy

118
00:09:18,639 --> 00:09:23,020
was flattered. Doubtless there was a certain struggle on a certain relief.

119
00:09:23,020 --> 00:09:27,760
The letter reached Gatsby while he was still at Oxford.

120
00:09:27,760 --> 00:09:33,818
It was dawn now on Long Island and we went about opening the rest of the windows downstairs,

121
00:09:33,818 --> 00:09:39,040
filling the house with gray turning gold turning light. The shadow of a tree fell

122
00:09:39,040 --> 00:09:44,738
abruptly across the dew and ghostly birds began to sing among the blue leaves.

123
00:09:44,738 --> 00:09:52,719
There was a slow, pleasant movement in the air, scarcely wind, promising a cool, lovely day.

124
00:09:52,719 --> 00:09:57,219
“I don’t think she ever loved him.” Gatsby turned around from a window and looked

125
00:09:57,219 --> 00:10:02,139
at me challengingly. “You must remember, old sport, she was very excited this

126
00:10:02,139 --> 00:10:06,398
afternoon. He told her those things in a way that frightened her— —that made it

127
00:10:06,398 --> 00:10:10,299
look as if I was some kind of cheap sharper. And the result was she hardly

128
00:10:10,299 --> 00:10:15,100
knew what she was saying.” He sat down gloomily. “Of course she might

129
00:10:15,100 --> 00:10:21,459
have loved him just for a minute, when they were first married— —and loved me more even then,

130
00:10:21,459 --> 00:10:28,359
do you see?” Suddenly he came out with a curious remark. “In any case,” he said,

131
00:10:28,359 --> 00:10:33,040
“it was just personal.” What could you make of that, except to

132
00:10:33,040 --> 00:10:37,840
suspect some intensity in his conception of the affair that couldn’t be measured?

133
00:10:37,840 --> 00:10:41,559
He came back from France when Tom and Daisy were still on their wedding trip,

134
00:10:41,559 --> 00:10:46,840
and made a miserable but irresistible journey to Louisville on the last of his army pay.

135
00:10:46,840 --> 00:10:50,859
He stayed there a week, walking the streets where their footsteps had

136
00:10:50,859 --> 00:10:55,120
clicked together through the November night and revisiting the out of the way

137
00:10:55,120 --> 00:10:58,659
places to which they had driven in her white car.

138
00:10:58,659 --> 00:11:03,639
Just as Daisy’s house had always seemed to him more mysterious and gay than

139
00:11:03,639 --> 00:11:09,100
other houses, so his idea of the city itself, even though she was gone from it,

140
00:11:09,100 --> 00:11:12,398
was pervaded with a melancholy beauty.

141
00:11:12,398 --> 00:11:17,439
He left feeling that if he had searched harder, he might have found her— —that he

142
00:11:17,439 --> 00:11:20,439
was leaving her behind.

143
00:11:20,439 --> 00:11:26,079
The day coach he was penniless now— —was hot. He went out to the open vestibule

144
00:11:26,079 --> 00:11:30,459
and sat down on a folding chair and the station slid away and the backs of

145
00:11:30,459 --> 00:11:33,040
unfamiliar buildings moved by.

146
00:11:33,040 --> 00:11:37,540
Then out into the Springfields where a yellow trolley raced them for a minute

147
00:11:37,540 --> 00:11:42,159
with people in it who might once have seen the pale magic of her face along

148
00:11:42,159 --> 00:11:43,959
the casual street.

149
00:11:43,959 --> 00:11:49,238
The track curved and now it was going away from the sun, which, as it sank lower,

150
00:11:49,238 --> 00:11:54,939
seemed to spread itself in benediction over the vanishing city where she had drawn her breath.

151
00:11:54,939 --> 00:12:01,000
He stretched out his hand desperately as if to snatch only a wisp of air, to save

152
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:05,139
a fragment of the spot that she had made lovely for him.

153
00:12:05,139 --> 00:12:08,199
But it was all going by too fast now for

154
00:12:08,199 --> 00:12:14,559
his blurred eyes and he knew that he had lost that part of it, the freshest and

155
00:12:14,559 --> 00:12:18,238
the best, forever.

156
00:12:19,478 --> 00:12:23,978
It was nine o’clock when we finished breakfast and went out on the porch. The

157
00:12:23,978 --> 00:12:29,439
night had made a sharp difference in the weather and there was an autumn flavor in the air.

158
00:12:29,439 --> 00:12:35,738
The gardener, the last one of Gatsby’s former servants, came to the foot of the steps.

159
00:12:35,738 --> 00:12:40,420
“I’m gonna drain the pool today, Mr. Gatsby leaves will start falling pretty soon,

160
00:12:40,420 --> 00:12:44,920
and then there’s always trouble with the pipes.” “Don’t do it today,”

161
00:12:44,920 --> 00:12:48,760
Gatsby answered. He turned to me apologetically.

162
00:12:48,760 --> 00:12:53,978
“You know, old sport, I’ve never used that pool all summer?”

163
00:12:53,978 --> 00:12:58,478
I looked at my watch and stood up 12 minutes to my train.”

164
00:12:58,478 --> 00:13:00,398
I didn’t want to go to the city.

165
00:13:00,398 --> 00:13:05,199
I wasn’t worth a decent stroke of work, but it was more than that—

166
00:13:05,199 --> 00:13:07,898
—I didn’t want to leave Gatsby.

167
00:13:07,898 --> 00:13:13,659
I missed that train, and then another, before I could get myself away.

168
00:13:13,659 --> 00:13:16,540
“I’ll call you up,” I said finally.

169
00:13:16,540 --> 00:13:23,260
“Do, old sport.” “I’ll call you about noon.” We walked slowly down the steps.

170
00:13:23,260 --> 00:13:27,520
“I suppose Daisy will call too.” He looked at me anxiously, as if he'd

171
00:13:27,520 --> 00:13:29,680
hoped I’d corroborate this.

172
00:13:29,680 --> 00:13:35,260
“I suppose so.” “Well, goodbye.” We shook hands and I started away.

173
00:13:35,260 --> 00:13:40,359
Just before I reached the hedge I remembered something and turned around.

174
00:13:40,359 --> 00:13:45,040
“They’re a rotten crowd,” I shouted across the lawn.

175
00:13:45,040 --> 00:13:49,020
“You’re worth the whole damn bunch put together.”

176
00:13:49,180 --> 00:13:53,439
I’ve always been glad I said that. It was the only compliment I ever gave him,

177
00:13:53,439 --> 00:13:57,040
because I disapproved of him from beginning to end.

178
00:13:57,040 --> 00:14:03,699
First he nodded politely, and then his face broke into that radiant and understanding smile,

179
00:14:03,699 --> 00:14:09,159
as if we’d been in ecstatic cahoots on that fact all the time.

180
00:14:09,159 --> 00:14:14,620
His gorgeous pink rag of a suit made a bright spot of color against the white steps,

181
00:14:14,620 --> 00:14:20,079
and I thought of the night when I first came to his ancestral home, three months before.

182
00:14:20,079 --> 00:14:26,559
The lawn and drive had been crowded with the faces of those who guessed at his corruption—

183
00:14:26,559 --> 00:14:34,959
—and he had stood on those steps, concealing his incorruptible dream, as he waved them goodbye.

184
00:14:34,959 --> 00:14:37,299
I thanked him for his hospitality.

185
00:14:37,299 --> 00:14:41,439
We were always thanking him for that— —I and the others.

186
00:14:41,439 --> 00:14:48,219
“Goodbye,” I called. “I enjoyed breakfast, Gatsby.” Up in the city, I tried for a while to

187
00:14:48,219 --> 00:14:53,859
list the quotations on an interminable amount of stock, then I fell asleep in my swivel chair

188
00:14:53,859 --> 00:14:59,920
Just before noon the phone woke me, and I started up with sweat breaking out on my forehead.

189
00:14:59,920 --> 00:15:04,359
It was Jordan Baker; she often called me up this hour because the uncertainty of

190
00:15:04,359 --> 00:15:08,079
her own movements between hotels and clubs and private houses made her hard

191
00:15:08,079 --> 00:15:10,238
to find in any other way.

192
00:15:10,238 --> 00:15:14,318
Usually her voice came over the wire as something fresh and cool,

193
00:15:14,318 --> 00:15:19,600
as if a divot from a green Golf Links had come sailing in at the office window,

194
00:15:19,600 --> 00:15:22,540
but this morning it seemed harsh and dry.

195
00:15:22,540 --> 00:15:25,659
“I’ve left Daisy’s house,” she said.

196
00:15:25,659 --> 00:15:29,978
“I’m at hamsted and I’m going down to Southampton this afternoon.”

197
00:15:29,978 --> 00:15:35,620
Probably it had been tactful to leave Daisy’s house, but the act annoyed me, and

198
00:15:35,620 --> 00:15:37,840
her next remark made me rigid.

199
00:15:37,840 --> 00:15:42,760
“You weren’t so nice to me last night.” “How could it have mattered then?”

200
00:15:42,760 --> 00:15:44,738
Silence for a moment.

201
00:15:44,738 --> 00:15:49,898
Then: “However— —I want to see you.” “I want to see you, too.”

202
00:15:49,898 --> 00:15:53,500
“Suppose I don’t go to Southampton, and come into town this afternoon?”

203
00:15:53,500 --> 00:15:58,959
“No— —I don’t think this afternoon.” “Very well.” “It’s impossible this afternoon.

204
00:15:58,959 --> 00:16:06,040
Various—” We talked like that for a while, and then abruptly we weren’t talking any longer.

205
00:16:06,040 --> 00:16:08,079
I don’t know which of us hung up with a

206
00:16:08,079 --> 00:16:10,540
sharp click, but I know I didn’t care.

207
00:16:10,540 --> 00:16:14,500
I couldn’t have talked to her across a tea table that day if I never talked to

208
00:16:14,500 --> 00:16:17,079
her again in this world.

209
00:16:17,079 --> 00:16:23,920
I called Gatsby’s house a few minutes later, but the line was busy. I tried four times;

210
00:16:23,920 --> 00:16:28,600
finally an exasperated central told me the wire was being kept open for long

211
00:16:28,600 --> 00:16:30,639
distance from Detroit.

212
00:16:30,639 --> 00:16:35,260
Taking out my timetable, I drew a small circle around the 350 train.

213
00:16:35,260 --> 00:16:38,379
Then I leaned back in my chair and tried to think.

214
00:16:38,379 --> 00:16:40,299
It was just noon.

215
00:16:40,299 --> 00:16:44,260
When I passed the ash heaps on the train that morning I had crossed deliberately

216
00:16:44,260 --> 00:16:46,359
to the other side of the car.

217
00:16:46,359 --> 00:16:50,318
I suppose there’d be a curious crowd around there all day with little boys

218
00:16:50,318 --> 00:16:55,120
searching for dark spots in the dust, and some garrulous man telling over and over

219
00:16:55,120 --> 00:16:59,500
what had happened, until it became less and less real even to him and he could

220
00:16:59,500 --> 00:17:04,719
tell it no longer, and Myrtle Wilson’s tragic achievement was forgotten.

221
00:17:04,719 --> 00:17:08,139
Now I want to go back a little and tell what happened at the garage after we

222
00:17:08,139 --> 00:17:10,180
left there the night before.

223
00:17:10,180 --> 00:17:14,079
They had difficulty in locating the sister, Catherine. She must have broken

224
00:17:14,079 --> 00:17:18,279
her rule against drinking that night, for when she arrived she was stupid with

225
00:17:18,279 --> 00:17:23,139
liquor and unable to understand that the ambulance had already gone to Flushing.

226
00:17:23,139 --> 00:17:27,219
When they convinced her of this, she immediately fainted, as if that was the

227
00:17:27,219 --> 00:17:31,840
intolerable part of the affair. Someone, kind or curious, took her in his car and

228
00:17:31,840 --> 00:17:36,040
drove her in the wake of her sister’s body. Until long after midnight a

229
00:17:36,040 --> 00:17:40,120
changing crowd lapped up against the front of the garage, while George Wilson

230
00:17:40,120 --> 00:17:43,600
rocked himself back and forth on the couch inside.

231
00:17:43,600 --> 00:17:48,159
For a while the door of the office was open, and everyone who came into the

232
00:17:48,159 --> 00:17:50,799
garage glanced irresistibly through it.

233
00:17:50,799 --> 00:17:54,159
Finally someone said it was a shame, and closed the door

234
00:17:54,159 --> 00:18:00,100
Nicholas and several other men were with him; first, four or five men, later two or

235
00:18:00,100 --> 00:18:05,439
three men. Still later Nicholas had to ask the last stranger to wait there 15

236
00:18:05,439 --> 00:18:10,779
minutes longer, while he went back to his own place and made a pot of coffee. After

237
00:18:10,779 --> 00:18:14,859
that, he stayed there alone with Wilson until dawn.

238
00:18:14,859 --> 00:18:19,898
About three o’clock the quality of Wilson’s incoherent muttering changed— —he

239
00:18:19,898 --> 00:18:24,219
grew quieter and began to talk about the yellow car. He announced that he had a

240
00:18:24,219 --> 00:18:28,719
way of finding out whom the yellow car belonged to, and then he blurted out that

241
00:18:28,719 --> 00:18:33,159
a couple of months ago his wife had come from the city with her face bruised and

242
00:18:33,159 --> 00:18:37,478
her nose swollen. But when he heard himself say this, he flinched and began

243
00:18:37,478 --> 00:18:45,879
to cry “Oh, my God!” again in his groaning voice Nicholas made a clumsy attempt to distract him.

244
00:18:45,879 --> 00:18:47,799
“How long have you been married, George?

245
00:18:47,799 --> 00:18:51,459
Come on there, try and sit still a minute, and answer my question.

246
00:18:51,459 --> 00:18:56,859
How long have you been married 12 years.” “Ever have any children?

247
00:18:56,859 --> 00:19:00,699
Come on, George, sit still— —I asked you a question do you ever have any children?”

248
00:19:00,699 --> 00:19:06,459
The hard brown beetles kept sudden against the dull light, and whenever Nicholas heard a car go

249
00:19:06,459 --> 00:19:10,299
tearing along the road outside it sounded to him like the car that hadn’t

250
00:19:10,299 --> 00:19:12,639
stopped a few hours before.

251
00:19:12,639 --> 00:19:16,719
He didn’t like to go into the garage, because the workbench was stained where

252
00:19:16,719 --> 00:19:21,340
the body had been lying, so he moved uncomfortably around the office—

253
00:19:21,340 --> 00:19:26,620
—he knew every object in it before morning— —and from time to time sat down beside

254
00:19:26,620 --> 00:19:29,978
Wilson trying to keep him more quiet.

255
00:19:29,978 --> 00:19:32,559
“Have you got a church you go to sometimes, George?

256
00:19:32,559 --> 00:19:35,620
Maybe even if you haven’t been there for a long time? Maybe I could call up the

257
00:19:35,620 --> 00:19:38,799
church and get a priest to come over and he could talk to you, see?”

258
00:19:38,799 --> 00:19:44,139
“Don’t belong to any.” “You ought to have a church, George, for times like this.

259
00:19:44,139 --> 00:19:48,520
You must have gone to church once. Didn’t you get married in a church? Listen,

260
00:19:48,520 --> 00:19:55,478
George, listen to me. Didn’t you get married in the church?” “That was a long time ago.”

261
00:19:55,478 --> 00:20:02,318
The effort of answering broke the rhythm of his rocking— —for a moment he was silent.

262
00:20:02,318 --> 00:20:08,500
Then the same half-knowing half bewildered look came back into his faded eyes.

263
00:20:08,500 --> 00:20:13,898
“Look in the drawer there,” he said, pointing at the desk. “Which drawer?”

264
00:20:13,898 --> 00:20:20,139
“That drawer— —that one Nicholas opened the drawer nearest his hand.

265
00:20:20,139 --> 00:20:25,959
There was nothing in it but a small, expensive dog leash made of leather and braided silver.

266
00:20:25,959 --> 00:20:28,238
It was apparently new.

267
00:20:28,238 --> 00:20:33,760
“This?” he inquired, holding it up. Wilson stared and nodded.

268
00:20:33,760 --> 00:20:38,139
“I found it yesterday afternoon. She tried to tell me about it, but I knew it was

269
00:20:38,139 --> 00:20:44,079
something funny.” “You mean your wife bought it?” “She had it wrapped in tissue paper on

270
00:20:44,079 --> 00:20:50,020
her bureau Nicholas didn’t see anything odd in that, and he gave Wilson a dozen reasons why

271
00:20:50,020 --> 00:20:54,818
his wife might have bought the dog leash But conceivably Wilson had heard some of

272
00:20:54,818 --> 00:20:59,439
these explanations before, from Myrtle, because he began saying

273
00:20:59,439 --> 00:21:07,719
“Oh, my God!” again in a whisper— —his comforter left several explanations in the air.

274
00:21:07,719 --> 00:21:14,260
“Then he killed her,” said Wilson. His mouth dropped open suddenly. “Who did?”

275
00:21:14,260 --> 00:21:20,079
“I have a way of finding out.” “You’re morbid, George,” said his friend.

276
00:21:20,079 --> 00:21:23,680
“This has been a strain to you and you don’t know what you’re saying.

277
00:21:23,680 --> 00:21:31,659
You’d better try to sit quiet till morning.” “He murdered her.” “It was an accident, George.”

278
00:21:31,659 --> 00:21:33,699
Wilson shook his head.

279
00:21:33,699 --> 00:21:40,738
His eyes narrowed and his mouth widened slightly with the ghost of a superior

280
00:21:40,959 --> 00:21:46,600
I know,” he said defiantly I am one of these trusting fellas and I don’t think

281
00:21:46,600 --> 00:21:51,219
any harm to anybody but when I get to know a thing I know it.

282
00:21:51,219 --> 00:21:53,920
It was the man in that car. She ran out

283
00:21:53,920 --> 00:21:59,859
to speak to him and he wouldn’t stop Nicholas had seen this too, but it hadn’t

284
00:21:59,859 --> 00:22:04,120
occurred to him that there was any special significance in it. He believed

285
00:22:04,120 --> 00:22:08,139
that Mrs. Wilson had been running away from her husband, rather than trying to

286
00:22:08,139 --> 00:22:10,659
stop any particular car.

287
00:22:10,659 --> 00:22:15,520
“How could she have been like that?” “She’s a deep one,” said Wilson, as if that

288
00:22:15,520 --> 00:22:20,859
answered the question uh He began to rock again, and Nicholas

289
00:22:20,859 --> 00:22:22,540
stood twisting the leash in his hand.

290
00:22:22,540 --> 00:22:29,500
“Maybe you got some friend that I could telephone for, George?” This was a forlorn hope—

291
00:22:29,500 --> 00:22:35,318
—he was almost sure that Wilson had no friend: there was not enough of him for his wife.

292
00:22:35,318 --> 00:22:39,279
He was glad a little later when he noticed a change in the room, a blue

293
00:22:39,279 --> 00:22:44,020
quickening by the windows and realized that dawn wasn’t far off.

294
00:22:44,020 --> 00:22:49,299
About five o’clock it was blue enough outside to snap off the light.

295
00:22:49,299 --> 00:22:54,939
Wilson’s glazed eyes turned out to the ash heaps where small gray clouds took

296
00:22:54,939 --> 00:23:00,760
on fantastic shape and scurried here and there in the faint dawn wind. “I spoke to

297
00:23:00,760 --> 00:23:05,079
her,” he muttered, after a long silence. “I told her she might fool me but she

298
00:23:05,079 --> 00:23:08,260
couldn’t fool God. I took her to the window”—

299
00:23:08,260 --> 00:23:12,699
”—with an effort he got up and walked to the rear window and leaned with his face

300
00:23:12,699 --> 00:23:16,478
pressed against it—“ —“and I said ‘God knows what you’ve been

301
00:23:16,478 --> 00:23:22,420
doing, everything you’ve been doing. You may fool me, but you can’t fool God!’

302
00:23:22,420 --> 00:23:27,639
Standing behind him Nicholas saw with a shock that he was looking at the eyes of

303
00:23:27,639 --> 00:23:32,680
Dr T..J eckelberg which had just emerged, pale and enormous,

304
00:23:32,680 --> 00:23:38,079
from the dissolving night. “God sees everything,” repeated Wilson. “That’s an

305
00:23:38,079 --> 00:23:40,420
advertisement Nicholas assured him.

306
00:23:40,420 --> 00:23:44,620
Something made him turn away from the window and look back into the room. But

307
00:23:44,620 --> 00:23:50,559
Wilson stood there a long time, his face close to the window pane, nodding into the twilight.

308
00:23:50,559 --> 00:23:56,318
By six o’clock Nicholas was born out, and grateful for the sound of a car stopping outside.

309
00:23:56,318 --> 00:24:00,579
It was one of the watchers of the night before who had promised to come back,

310
00:24:00,579 --> 00:24:04,898
so he cooked breakfast for three, which she and the other man ate together.

311
00:24:04,898 --> 00:24:10,779
Wilson was quieter now, and Nicholas went home to sleep; when he awoke four hours later and

312
00:24:10,779 --> 00:24:15,040
hurried back to the garage, Wilson was gone.

313
00:24:15,040 --> 00:24:20,318
His movements— —he was on foot all the time— —were afterward traced to Port

314
00:24:20,318 --> 00:24:25,478
Roosevelt and then to gads Hill, where he bought a sandwich that he didn’t eat, and

315
00:24:25,478 --> 00:24:26,799
a cup of coffee.

316
00:24:26,799 --> 00:24:30,579
He must have been tired and walking slowly, for he didn’t reach God's Hill

317
00:24:30,579 --> 00:24:35,620
until noon. Thus far there was no difficulty in accounting for his time

318
00:24:35,620 --> 00:24:41,199
they were boys who had seen a man “acting sort of crazy,” and motorists at whom he

319
00:24:41,199 --> 00:24:44,318
stared oddly from the side of the road.

320
00:24:44,318 --> 00:24:47,680
Then for three hours he disappeared from view.

321
00:24:47,680 --> 00:24:52,478
The police, on the strength of what he said to Nicholas that he “had a way of

322
00:24:52,478 --> 00:24:58,000
finding out,” supposed that he spent that time going from garage to garage they

323
00:24:58,000 --> 00:25:01,059
are about inquiring for a yellow car.

324
00:25:01,059 --> 00:25:07,120
On the other hand, no garage man who had seen him ever came forward, and perhaps

325
00:25:07,120 --> 00:25:11,199
he had an easier, surer way of finding out what he wanted to know.

326
00:25:11,199 --> 00:25:15,760
By half past two he was in West Egg, where he asked someone the way to

327
00:25:15,760 --> 00:25:17,318
Gatsby’s house.

328
00:25:17,318 --> 00:25:21,818
So by that time he knew Gatsby’s name.

329
00:25:21,818 --> 00:25:26,738
At two o’clock Gatsby put on his bathing suit and left word with the butler that

330
00:25:26,738 --> 00:25:30,639
if anyone phoned word was to be brought to him at the pool.

331
00:25:30,639 --> 00:25:36,219
He stopped to the garage for a pneumatic mattress that had amused his guests during the summer,

332
00:25:36,219 --> 00:25:38,859
and the chauffeur helped him pump it up.

333
00:25:38,859 --> 00:25:44,260
Then he gave instructions that the open car wasn’t to be taken out under any circumstances—

334
00:25:44,260 --> 00:25:48,159
—and this was strange, because the front right fender needed repair.

335
00:25:48,159 --> 00:25:51,760
Gatsby shouldered the mattress and started for the pool.

336
00:25:51,760 --> 00:25:55,719
Once he stopped and shifted it a little, and the chauffeur asked him if he needed

337
00:25:55,719 --> 00:26:01,600
help, but he shook his head and in a moment disappeared among the yellowing trees.

338
00:26:01,600 --> 00:26:06,159
No telephone message arrived, but the butler went without his sleep and waited

339
00:26:06,159 --> 00:26:10,779
for it until four o’clock— —until long after there was anyone to give it to if

340
00:26:10,779 --> 00:26:12,279
it came.

341
00:26:12,279 --> 00:26:16,779
I have an idea that Gatsby himself didn’t believe it would come, and perhaps

342
00:26:16,779 --> 00:26:21,939
he no longer cared. If that was true he must have felt that he had lost the old

343
00:26:21,939 --> 00:26:27,639
warm world, paid a high price for living too long with a single dream.

344
00:26:27,639 --> 00:26:32,559
He must have looked up at an unfamiliar sky through frightening leaves and

345
00:26:32,559 --> 00:26:36,340
shivered as he found what a grotesque thing or rose is

346
00:26:36,340 --> 00:26:40,959
and how raw the sunlight was upon the scarcely created grass.

347
00:26:40,959 --> 00:26:47,500
A new world, material without being real, where poor ghosts, breathing dreams like

348
00:26:47,500 --> 00:26:53,318
air, drifted fortuitously about… like that ashen, fantastic figure gliding

349
00:26:53,318 --> 00:26:56,318
toward him through the amorphous trees.

350
00:26:56,318 --> 00:27:02,260
The chauffeur— —he was one of wolfsheim's protege heard the shots

351
00:27:02,260 --> 00:27:07,299
afterward he could only say that he hadn’t thought anything much about them.

352
00:27:07,299 --> 00:27:11,680
I drove from the station directly to Gatsby’s house and my rushing anxiously

353
00:27:11,680 --> 00:27:16,359
up the front steps was the first thing that alarmed anyone.

354
00:27:16,359 --> 00:27:20,260
But they knew then, I firmly believe.

355
00:27:20,260 --> 00:27:27,520
With scarcely a word said, four of us, the chauffeur, butler, gardener, and I hurried

356
00:27:27,520 --> 00:27:29,139
down to the pool.

357
00:27:29,139 --> 00:27:35,318
There is a faint, barely perceptible movement of the water as the fresh flow from one end

358
00:27:35,318 --> 00:27:39,340
urged its way towards the drain at the other. With little ripples that were

359
00:27:39,340 --> 00:27:44,159
hardly the shadows of waves, the laden mattress moved irregularly down the pool.

360
00:27:44,159 --> 00:27:49,059
A small gust of wind that scarcely corrugated the surface was enough to

361
00:27:49,059 --> 00:27:54,579
disturb its accidental course with its accidental burden. The touch of a cluster

362
00:27:54,579 --> 00:27:59,859
of leaves revolved it slowly, tracing, like the leg of a compass a thin red

363
00:27:59,859 --> 00:28:02,260
circle in the water.

364
00:28:02,260 --> 00:28:06,459
It was after we started with Gatsby towards the house that the gardener saw

365
00:28:06,459 --> 00:28:12,000
Wilson’s body a little way off in the grass, and the holocaust was complete
